# coordinator runtime artifacts
coordinator.out
docs-site-build/

# local notes, not for the repo
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md
benchmarks/matching/cache/
# Per-machine Claude Code state (settings.local.json, local skills). Nothing in
# here belongs to the project, and leaving it untracked-but-unignored meant it sat
# in every `git status` and one `git add -A` from being committed, which is the
# same trap the audit-notes rule below exists to close.
.claude/

# scratch design assets — kept local, never committed
landing-mockups/
logo-prompts.txt

# internal AI-agent audit/planning notes — point-in-time snapshots, not docs for
# readers; kept local, never committed or deployed
docs/internal/
# …and the same class of file wherever it lands. CLAUDE.md has always said these
# must never reach the public mirror, but until 2026-07-28 only `docs/internal/`
# was actually ignored: a root-level GPT56-AUDIT.md sat untracked-and-unignored,
# one `git add -A` away from being committed and then published. A rule enforced
# only by whoever remembers to check `git status` is not enforced.
*-AUDIT.md
session-*.md

# playwright artifacts
e2e/test-results/
RELEASE_ID

# Local Tier-2 test stack config (see docs/E2E-TESTING-GUIDE.md) — machine-specific
packages/coordinator/local-test.toml

# The model watcher's rolling baseline — mutable state, not a pinned snapshot
# (that's venice-models.json, which IS committed because published cost figures
# are computed against it). Tracking this would dirty the tree on every hourly
# run; `node watch-new-models.mjs --init` seeds a fresh one.
benchmarks/matching/.model-watch.json

# Local verification output (e.g. a docs-site build rendered to eyeball links)
tmp/

# Prompt-tuning fixtures built from REAL attendees of a REAL event (see
# benchmarks/matching/private/README.md). The public benchmark runs on invented
# personas precisely so it can be published; this one cannot be, and the
# working tree of `main` is what gets copied onto the public mirror by the sync
# ritual in CLAUDE.md. Ignoring the whole directory is the enforcement — the
# alternative is trusting that nobody ever runs `git add -A` in a hurry.
benchmarks/matching/private/
